DATA = "# Frozen security implementation prompt — ARCHSPSCGG_12344_R1\n\n## Mission and source-grounded boundaries\nImplement Windows BYOK credential persistence using Windows Credential Manager, not new files. Preserve Settings profile metadata and secret separation, save/reopen/restart/login, replacement/deletion, macOS Keychain, Linux Secret Service, local CLI and cloud modes. Explicit BYOK must never silently fall back to paid cloud execution. Windows failures must reject the operation with a safe message. Do not invent native dependencies or claim native Windows verification on Linux.\n\nRead `apps/daemon/src/byok/credential-service.ts`: `ByokSecretBackend` (`available/set/get/delete`), `createPlatformByokSecretBackend`, `ByokCredentialService.upsert/resolve/delete`, metadata `dataDir/byok/profiles.json`, `RetiredWindowsSecretCleanupBackend`. Review `apps/daemon/src/routes/byok-credentials.ts` authorization, `routes/runs.ts` BYOK admission, Settings UI, runtime and tests before modifying their contracts. `apps/daemon/package.json` has Node ~24, node-pty but no native Credential Manager binding; do not add speculative dependency. The daemon derives `dataDir` from app configuration (including environment `OD_DATA_DIR`), never a BYOK HTTP request; metadata paths remain application-managed. Treat environment/storage-root control as a deployment boundary, not a profile-ID permission.\n\n## Design comparison and decision\n1. Fixed-location DPAPI vault: one dataDir-relative fixed file, profile IDs only map keys inside ciphertext; requires a safely implemented DPAPI binding, multi-process locking, bounds, atomic replace, permission discipline, corruption management, backup strategy and migration. No verified native DPAPI module is available in supplied deps. A PowerShell DPAPI helper would require trusted-process management plus a filesystem transaction layer. Reject due to larger integrity/race surface.\n2. **Selected: Credential Manager generic credentials via real Advapi32 CredWriteW/CredReadW/CredDeleteW P/Invoke**, invoked from Windows PowerShell (`powershell.exe`, `-NoProfile`, `-NonInteractive`, fixed encoded program); PowerShell uses C# `Add-Type` with literal, reviewed platform P/Invoke definitions. Structured bounded JSON on stdin contains the secret and logical profile ID; the only command argument containing program data is static code (never request data). Generic credential target = fixed app namespace plus validated logical ID. The OS manages storage and user security context; use `CRED_PERSIST_LOCAL_MACHINE` so a user can access saved entries after logging in again on that machine. No credential-storage filesystem calls in the new backend. Missing or denied PowerShell/Cred APIs fail closed; never fall back to plaintext. The PowerShell process and current user are trusted OS boundaries; a compromised same-user process can read Credential Manager entries (same threat model as existing keychain backends).\n\n## Dataflow and path-provenance proof\n| Source | Logical identifier | Decision | Sink/path authority |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| HTTP profile id, validated by `assertProfileId` | profile ID | logical target under fixed CredMan namespace | `CredWriteW/ReadW/DeleteW`; **no application filesystem path** |\n| HTTP API key | only credential bytes | bounded JSON on child stdin | CredMan encrypted OS-managed entry, never argv or shell interpolation |\n| Existing trusted daemon `dataDir` | none | metadata only | existing fixed `byok/profiles.json`, fixed-root randomized metadata temp |\n| Existing legacy delete | validated profile ID | compatibility cleanup only | preexisting `dataDir/byok/secrets/<id>.bin`, NOT used for new persistence |\n| Fixed script constant | none | encoded PowerShell program argument | process execution, no filesystem path depending on identity |\n\nA1: No newly introduced file path (including temp, lock, cache, backup) may depend on identifiers, transformed identifiers or secrets. New CredMan backend uses no file operations. A2: distinct target names isolate credentials; reject malformed IDs and avoid target collision. A3: actual OS Cred APIs only, error/size checks; no homebrew crypto or fallback. A4: never put secret/request data into process args, shell text, logs, telemetry, persisted metadata; fixed program and stdin transport, capped stdout, timeout and kill, clean error messages. A5: preserve metadata rollback and all lifecycle/platform modes; failures do not return success. A6: instrument path creation and assert two distinct valid IDs have identical *new credential-storage filesystem path sets* (both empty), while two different CredMan targets retain two different secrets; separately test retired cleanup and label its legacy exception.\n\n## Implementation instructions\nAdd one Windows Credential Manager backend (possibly in separate TypeScript module) selected when platform is `win32`. Validate profile"
